title: 'Reserve scheduler capacity for privileged governance alarms and enactments'

doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      Adds a priority-based reservation to `pallet-scheduler` and uses it in `pallet-referenda`,
      so that privileged governance keeps scheduling capacity when a block's agenda is contended.

      Previously `pallet-referenda` admitted alarms and enactments by agenda count alone. When a
      target block's agenda was already at `MaxScheduledPerBlock`, scheduling failed, and that
      failure was neither reported nor retried: `set_alarm` fired a `debug_assert!` and stored
      `alarm = None`, leaving a referendum that is never serviced, and enactment scheduling
      discarded its result.

      Changes:
      - `pallet-scheduler` gains a `PriorityReserve` config constant. Once a block's agenda
        holds `MaxScheduledPerBlock - PriorityReserve` tasks, only tasks with priority at most
        `RESERVED_PRIORITY_THRESHOLD` (62) may take the remaining reserved slots; lower-priority
        tasks are rejected with `Exhausted`. `PriorityReserve = 0` preserves the previous
        behaviour.
      - `pallet-referenda` no longer asserts on a failed alarm schedule. `set_alarm` returns
        `None`, and `submit` propagates this as the new `Error::AlarmScheduleFailed`, reverting
        its state instead of storing a referendum that will never be serviced.
      - `TrackInfo` gains an `alarm_priority` field. A track's alarms and enactments use that
        priority only after the decision deposit has been posted. Before the deposit the
        least-urgent priority applies. Only privileged tracks are given a reserved-band
        `alarm_priority`, so the reserved slots are reachable only by a privileged referendum
        that has committed its decision deposit.

      The per-block agenda remains finite, so this does not remove contention for agenda space.
      It reserves a portion of that space for privileged tracks whose referenda have committed
      their decision deposit.

      Runtimes must set `type PriorityReserve` on their scheduler config and give each referenda
      track an `alarm_priority` (`128` reproduces the previous, unreserved behaviour).

crates:
  - name: pallet-scheduler
    bump: major
  - name: pallet-referenda
    bump: major
